,"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":37,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"I built a queue-scoring controller by hand that behaves like the tuned actuated controller, and turned it into the network weights. All three files are in `work/`. Nothing was simulated, so the test predictions are reasoned estimates only.\n\n**What I built:** every 5 s, each phase gets a score in vehicles:\n- 1 per halting car, 0.5 per moving car on the stop-line lanes, 0.1 per moving car upstream.\n- The shared upstream lane counts 0.65 toward the left phase, because its queues mostly come from left-turn pocket spillback.\n- The current phase gets a 0.6 bonus, set below 1 so a single waiting car always wins a switch.\n\nThe decision is the highest score. In the network, 12 units in each layer carry this score through the same increasing curve for every phase, so the network picks exactly what the rule picks. The other 52 units per layer keep their random initial weights.\n\n**Trainability (on `states.npz`):** median max prob 0.909, share above 0.99 0.221, max |logit| 8.56, saturation 0.004 / 0, no dead units. All three requirements are met.\n\n**Checks:**\n- The network matches the rule on 99.93% of recorded states; the 6 differences are exact ties.\n- It agrees with the actuated controller on 89.5% of decisions that change the signal.\n- It matches the rule on 99.9% of 20,000 synthetic heavy-queue states.\n- All 8 hand-made situations give the intended phase, including a lone car during the final drain.\n\n**Test predictions (veh-h):** low 34.5, nominal 45.0, high 56.6, about the actuated level, give or take 2.","ttft_ms":1022,"type":"result","duration_ms":1041795,"uuid":"44362a98-802e-420d-93ae-bea1e95f3093","ttft_stream_ms":880,"time_to_request_ms":47,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":3,"system_prompt":5,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":1,"input_attachments":7,"input_hooks":1,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":4,"tool_pool_refresh":2,"init_m